Expose a read-only, identity-bound membership view of the worker registry at
GET /observer/endpoints, for external confirmation of who would actually
answer.

THE PURPOSE. The router's existing surfaces answer a different question than
the one external confirmation asks. /list_workers and /workers report the
registry; the request path additionally applies health and circuit-breaker
filters on the way to each request, so a registered worker that the selection
would never pick is invisible in a listing and yet occupies a place in the
pool's bookkeeping. Confirmation needs the selection-time view: per worker, the
same predicates the dispatcher evaluates, evaluated by the same accessor calls
-- is_healthy() and circuit_breaker().can_execute(), whose conjunction
is_available() is exactly what the request path consults
(src/core/worker.rs:77-82).

READ-ONLY, AND WHY THAT IS THE WHOLE CONTRACT. The handler contains no policy
and no mutation: it iterates the registry's public snapshot
(WorkerRegistry::get_all_with_ids), reads is_healthy(), circuit_breaker().state()
and can_execute() off each live worker, and serializes. It never takes a
write path, never calls add/withdraw/record_outcome, and holds no configuration.
A patch that could nudge selection would not be an observer; this one contains
nothing that could.

IDENTITY BOUNDARY. The registry is URL-keyed and the router does not know
Kubernetes Pod identities, so the payload carries the URL, its port, the role
name (regular/prefill/decode from worker_type()), and the URL's host as
`pod_hint` -- a convenience echo, never an authoritative identity. Binding an
endpoint to a Pod UID is performed by the operator at collection time from its
own Kubernetes state; the design this patch implements assigns that binding to
the collector precisely so the router never grows an invented native identity
label.

FRESHNESS. `boot_generation` is the process start time in milliseconds:
constant within a process, different after every restart, so a consumer can
tell a restarted router from a converging one. `now_ms` is the response time.
`registry_revision` is a process-local counter that increments whenever the
serialized worker array changes (deterministic URL ordering makes the array
comparable), so equal consecutive revisions mean the registry content is
unchanged between them.

PLACEMENT. The handler is a new module (src/observer.rs) wired with one mod
declaration and one route line in the admin route group, next to /list_workers.
No existing handler, middleware, or policy file is modified.

Written against vllm-project/router v0.1.15 on top of
0001-mooncake-lazy-bootstrap-lookup.patch and
0002-discovery-readiness-and-deselect-removal.patch.
